\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Here is the translation of the provided Fortran code into Java.\
A few key points about this translation:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Modules to Classes:
\f0\b0  Each Fortran 
\f2\fs26 MODULE
\f0\fs24  is translated into a Java 
\f2\fs26 public class
\f0\fs24 .\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Global Variables:
\f0\b0  Variables in Fortran modules (like 
\f2\fs26 common_globals
\f0\fs24 ) are translated as 
\f2\fs26 public static
\f0\fs24  fields in the corresponding Java class, making them globally accessible.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f3\b\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 resize
\f1\fs24  Subroutines:
\f0\b0  In Fortran, 
\f2\fs26 allocatable
\f0\fs24  arrays are passed by reference, allowing the subroutine to change the caller's array. Java passes array references by value, so you 
\f1\b cannot
\f0\b0  change the caller's original reference. The standard Java pattern, used here, is to 
\f1\b return the new array
\f0\b0 . The caller must then assign this returned value, e.g., 
\f2\fs26 myArray = Resize.resize_double1(myArray, 100);
\f0\fs24 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Array Indexing:
\f0\b0  Fortran is 1-indexed by default, while Java is 0-indexed.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl1
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Allocatable Arrays:
\f0\b0  The 
\f2\fs26 resize
\f0\fs24  methods create 0-indexed arrays. The copy logic (using 
\f2\fs26 System.arraycopy
\f0\fs24 ) correctly copies from the start (index 0) of the old array to the start of the new one.\
\ls1\ilvl1
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Fixed-Size Arrays:
\f0\b0  For fixed-size arrays (like 
\f2\fs26 ccc2p0(6,6)
\f0\fs24  in 
\f2\fs26 common_globals
\f0\fs24 ), the Java array is declared as 
\f2\fs26 new double[7][7]
\f0\fs24 . This intentionally wastes a small amount of memory (at index 0) to allow the rest of the code to be ported using 1-based indexing (
\f2\fs26 [1]
\f0\fs24  to 
\f2\fs26 [6]
\f0\fs24 ) without modification, which is a common and safer porting strategy.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f3\b\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 resize_double0
\f1\fs24 :
\f0\b0  The Fortran 
\f2\fs26 allocate(var(n1_0:n1, ...))
\f0\fs24  syntax (using arbitrary lower bounds) is 
\f1\b not supported in Java
\f0\b0 . The translated Java method 
\f2\fs26 resize_double0
\f0\fs24  creates a 0-indexed array with the correct 
\f4\i length
\f0\i0  (e.g., 
\f2\fs26 n1 - n1_0 + 1
\f0\fs24 ). Any code that 
\f4\i uses
\f0\i0  this array must be modified to map the Fortran index 
\f2\fs26 i
\f0\fs24  to the Java index 
\f2\fs26 i - n1_0
\f0\fs24 . A detailed note is included in the code.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
